\chapter{Alcubierre-Class Metric Implementation}
\label{chap:alcubierre}

\section{Introduction}
The Alcubierre warp metric enables spacetime translation without violating
local speed-of-light constraints. This chapter describes the shaping function,
curvature gradients, and stability conditions used in the engine.

\section{Warp Metric Definition}
The Alcubierre line element is:
\begin{equation}
    ds^2 = -c^2 dt^2
    + [dx - v_s(t) f(r_s) dt]^2
    + dy^2 + dz^2,
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $v_s(t)$ is the bubble velocity,
    \item $f(r_s)$ is the shaping function,
    \item $r_s$ is the distance to the bubble center.
\end{itemize}

\section{Shaping Function}
The shaping function is:
\begin{equation}
    f(r_s)
    = \frac{\tanh[\sigma(r_s + R)] - \tanh[\sigma(r_s - R)]}
           {2\tanh(\sigma R)},
\end{equation}
with:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $R$ bubble radius,
    \item $\sigma$ steepness parameter.
\end{itemize}

\section{Curvature Gradients}
The metric solver computes:
\begin{equation}
    G_{\mu\nu}(t+\Delta t)
    = G_{\mu\nu}(t)
    + \Delta t \cdot \partial_t G_{\mu\nu},
\end{equation}
with FP64 precision.

\section{Energy Density Requirements}
Warp metrics require:
\begin{equation}
    \rho < 0,
\end{equation}
which is provided by negative mass fields.

\section{Stability Conditions}
Bubble stability requires:
\begin{equation}
    |\nabla f(r_s)| < \kappa,
\end{equation}
with $\kappa$ determined by curvature thresholds.

\section{Conclusion}
This implementation enables stable warp bubble formation within the sovereign
physics engine, supported by negative mass and exotic energy densities.